a number called gen_envs is 0.

a number called gen_flags is 0.

a boolean called gen_uses_flag_reader is true.

(Plan 327 Part A: the argv the harness will hand this program, and what
 the program should therefore see. The generator CHOOSES the arguments,
 so for once it knows the expected values at generation time — which is
 the deferred output-oracle problem already solved in one corner. A
 program given a valid invocation asserts its flags parsed to exactly
 what was passed and Exits with a distinct code if not, so a wrong
 answer becomes a detectable one without any reference implementation.

 gen_argv is read by loop_gen and handed to BOTH of the oracle's runs.
 If the two runs ever got different argv, the difference between them
 would BE the input and every program would be a false nondeterminism
 finding.

 gen_asserts_argv is false for the hostile shapes, where the point is
 that the program must not crash rather than that it must agree with a
 prediction — a malformed number raises and yields 0, a missing value
 leaves the flag empty, and an unknown flag is not consumed at all.
 Asserting on those would be asserting on behaviour the fuzzer is
 supposed to be QUESTIONING.)

a list called gen_argv is [].

a text called gen_expected_label is "".

a number called gen_expected_count is 0.

a boolean called gen_expects_on is false.

a boolean called gen_asserts_argv is false.

a number called gen_reader_calls is 0.

(--- The ledger's environment leaves, batch A ---------------------------

 docs/ledger/environment.md maps LANGUAGE.md:4471-4545 into numbered
 claims; everything below puts one of those claims on trial and ASSERTS
 its documented result, the same way the buffer leaves in gen_buffers.vox
 do: a failing assertion prints

     ASSERT ENV-06: expected 32 got -1

 and exits 95, which loop_gen.vox classifies as a wrong-value finding
 carrying that line. Every shape below was run against the real compiler
 before it was encoded - the probes are in vf_scratch/probes/ of the
 batch's own worktree and its report lists what each one printed.

 Rows covered here: ENV-02, ENV-03, ENV-04, ENV-05, ENV-06 (with ENV-07
 and ENV-09 folded into it), ENV-10, ENV-11.

 What the section gives a generator to assert with, and what it does
 not. The generator does not choose the child's environment - there is
 an argv oracle ('gen build argv', below) but no environment companion -
 so no leaf here asserts WHAT a variable holds. What it can assert is
 agreement: LANGUAGE.md:4477-4483 documents count, empty, first, last and
 the named lookup as properties of ONE environment, so two readings of
 the same property cannot disagree, and `count` cannot say "none" while
 `empty` says "some". A text value read into a `text` and the same value
 read into a `buffer` (LANGUAGE.md's buffer declaration takes any
 text-valued expression - the Variables and File I/O sections' worked
 examples, `a buffer called b is "hello"` and `a buffer called b is
 <text name>`) are two readings of one value, and the buffer's `size`
 and `empty` (LANGUAGE.md:3216-3217) are how a program measures them -
 a `text` has no size property of its own. The one thing the generator
 DOES control is absence: a name it invents under VOXFUZZ_UNSET_ is set
 by nobody, so `exists` (LANGUAGE.md:4512-4517) on it is asserted false.

 Privacy, inherited from the leaf this block replaces: a generated
 program never prints a raw environment value - the first version of
 that leaf put a host's PATH into a checked-in fixture. Every read below
 is printed as a size, an `empty`, or `as a boolean`.)

(Names. A habit of `env1`, `env2` puts the same identifier in every
 program; CLAUDE.md wants a citation for that and there is none. These
 are the words a generated program's environment readings are actually
 called - things you would read off a wall or a door. A word is claimed
 at most once per program; a numeric suffix appears only if a program
 asks for more names than there are words.)

a list called gen_environment_words is ["the nameplate", "the doormat", "the noticeboard", "the roll call", "the headcount", "the signpost", "the waypoint", "the placard", "the lantern", "the compass", "the opening entry", "the closing entry", "the second reading", "the first reading", "the label", "the badge", "the locale", "the surroundings", "the lodging", "the workshop", "the porch light", "the letterbox", "the doorbell", "the welcome mat", "the hallway", "the coat hook", "the key rack", "the visitor book", "the house number", "the street sign"].

a list called gen_environment_word_taken is [].

a number called gen_environment_names is 0.

a text called gen_environment_last_word is "".

(Called once per program from 'gen program', beside 'gen buffer names
 reset': the claimed-word marks are per program, not per campaign.)

To 'gen environment names reset'.
    Set gen_environment_names to 0.
    Set gen_environment_word_taken to [].
    a number called 'how many words' is gen_environment_words's length.
    a number called 'the word position' is 0.
    While 'the word position' is less than 'how many words',
        append 0 to gen_environment_word_taken,
        Set 'the word position' to 'the word position' add 1.

(The stem of a fresh name - `'the doormat` - quoted because every word
 here has a space in it. 'gen buffer reference' closes the quote and
 'gen buffer property' hangs a property off it; both are plain text
 helpers that know nothing about buffers despite their names.)

To 'gen environment name'.
    Set gen_environment_names to gen_environment_names add 1.
    a number called 'how many words' is gen_environment_words's length.
    a number called 'the draw' is 'rng below' of 'how many words'.
    a number called 'the chosen word number' is 0.
    a number called 'the steps taken' is 0.
    While 'the steps taken' is less than 'how many words',
        a number called 'the offset sum' is 'the draw' add 'the steps taken',
        a number called 'the wrapped' is 'the offset sum' modulo 'how many words',
        a number called 'the candidate' is 'the wrapped' add 1,
        a number called 'the candidate is taken' is element 'the candidate' of gen_environment_word_taken,
        If 'the candidate is taken' is 0 and 'the chosen word number' is 0 then,
            Set 'the chosen word number' to 'the candidate'.
        Set 'the steps taken' to 'the steps taken' add 1.
    If 'the chosen word number' is 0 then,
        a number called 'the fallback draw' is 'rng below' of 'how many words',
        a number called 'the fallback number' is 'the fallback draw' add 1,
        a text called 'the fallback word' is element 'the fallback number' of gen_environment_words,
        Set gen_environment_last_word to "{'the fallback word'} {gen_environment_names}",
        Return a text, "'{gen_environment_last_word}".
    Set element 'the chosen word number' of gen_environment_word_taken to 1.
    a text called 'the word' is element 'the chosen word number' of gen_environment_words.
    Set gen_environment_last_word to 'the word'.
    Return a text, "'{'the word'}".

(A companion name for the reading just named - a flag, a tally - built
 from the same word so the program still reads aloud.)

To 'gen environment companion' with a text called role.
